## Description

`Template().fromDockerfile()` silently drops intermediate sources from multi-source `COPY` instructions. Only the first source is copied; all others are ignored without warning.

## Reproduction

```js
import { Template } from "e2b";

const dockerfile = `FROM ubuntu:22.04
COPY file1.txt file2.txt /dest/`;

const t = Template().fromDockerfile(dockerfile);
const json = JSON.parse(await t.toJSON());

// Expected: two COPY steps (file1.txt → /dest/, file2.txt → /dest/)
//   or one COPY step with both sources
// Actual: one COPY step with only file1.txt → /dest/
console.log(json.steps.filter(s => s.type === "COPY"));
// [{ type: "COPY", args: ["file1.txt", "/dest/", "", ""], force: false }]
```

`file2.txt` is silently dropped.

## Root cause

`handleCopyInstruction` takes `args[0]` as source and `args[args.length - 1]` as destination, ignoring everything in between:

```js
// node_modules/e2b/dist/index.mjs ~line 5025
function handleCopyInstruction(instruction, templateBuilder) {
  const argumentsData = instruction.getArguments();
  if (argumentsData && argumentsData.length >= 2) {
    const src = argumentsData[0].getValue();                       // only first
    const dest = argumentsData[argumentsData.length - 1].getValue(); // last
    templateBuilder.copy(src, dest, { user });
  }
}
```

## Impact

This is a silent data loss — builds succeed but produce broken images. In our case, `COPY gateway/package.json gateway/package-lock.json /opt/qumis/gateway/` resulted in only `package.json` being copied, causing `npm ci` to fail at runtime with a confusing "no package-lock.json" error.

## Expected behavior

Either:
1. Emit a separate `copy()` call for each source (matching Docker semantics), or
2. Throw an error telling the user multi-source COPY is not supported

## Workaround

Split into one COPY per source file:

```dockerfile
COPY gateway/package.json /opt/qumis/gateway/
COPY gateway/package-lock.json /opt/qumis/gateway/
```

